$schema: https://usm.dev/schema/v1.json
$id: smith-gray/token-revocation
$type: feature
$version: 1
$last_updated: "2026-09-22"
summary: |
  Revocation invalidates a capability token immediately, without waiting for its TTL to expire. A
  jti denylist checked on every resolve provides the kill switch; short TTL bounds the residual
  window for anything already in flight. Later phases add device binding, so a lost hardware
  token is revoked by serial.
$system: smith-gray/system
$service: smith-gray/control-plane
status: built
intent: |
  Short TTLs alone leave an unacceptable window when a token is known to be leaked — minutes to
  hours of live authority. A denylist gives an operator the immediate kill switch that incident
  response requires, and the two mechanisms together cover both the slow case (expiry) and the
  urgent one (revocation).
flows:
  - id: revoke-token
    name: Revoke a capability token
    description: An operator invalidates a specific token by its unique id.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: receive
        target: Operator issues a revoke request for a jti or object id.
      - id: s2
        action: validate
        target: Control plane checks the operator's authority to revoke that object or token.
      - id: s3
        action: record
        target: The jti is added to the denylist and the revoke is recorded in the audit log.
      - id: s4
        action: observe
        target: Every subsequent resolve attempt with that jti is denied.
  - id: revoke-device
    name: Revoke a device-bound token (later phase)
    description: A lost hardware token is revoked by serial.
    steps:
      - id: s1
        action: receive
        target: Operator revokes a device serial or its associated binding.
      - id: s2
        action: record
        target: The device binding is invalidated at the authority.
      - id: s3
        action: observe
        target: Attestation fails for that device on the next unwrap attempt.
contracts:
  - id: immediate-effect
    description: Revocation must take effect without waiting for token expiry.
    must_have:
      - The denylist is consulted on every resolve
      - No caching path can serve a revoked token
      - Effect is observable on the next resolve attempt
  - id: revoke-is-audited
    description: Revocation is itself an auditable event.
    must_have:
      - The revoking principal, target, and timestamp are recorded
      - The audit record survives in the append-only log
      - Revocations are queryable alongside resolve history
  - id: device-revocation-by-serial
    description: In the hardware phase, a lost device can be revoked by its serial.
    must_have:
      - Device identity is bound at provisioning
      - Revocation invalidates the binding at the authority
      - No re-provisioning of the same serial without operator action
tests:
  - id: revoked-token-denied-on-next-resolve
    flow: revoke-token
    setup:
      ttl_seconds: 3600
      revoke_before_use: true
    expect:
      - assertion: the next resolve with that jti is denied
      - assertion: denial occurs well before expiry
    contracts:
      - immediate-effect
  - id: revocation-recorded
    flow: revoke-token
    setup:
      valid_token: true
    expect:
      - assertion: an audit record exists for the revocation
      - assertion: it names the revoking principal and the target
    contracts:
      - revoke-is-audited
  - id: no-cache-serves-revoked-token
    flow: revoke-token
    setup:
      resolved_once_before_revocation: true
    expect:
      - assertion: a previously successful resolve does not make the token reusable
      - assertion: no in-memory cache bypasses the denylist
    contracts:
      - immediate-effect
decisions:
  - id: denylist-plus-ttl
    decision: Use a jti denylist alongside short TTLs rather than one or the other.
    rationale: >-
      TTL handles the unattended case cheaply and bounds worst-case exposure; the denylist handles
      the attended, urgent case. Neither alone is sufficient — long TTLs make leaks linger, and
      denylists alone still require knowing about the leak.
    status: accepted
  - id: authoritative-check-at-resolve
    decision: Check revocation at resolve time in the sidecar rather than trusting token validity alone.
    rationale: >-
      The sidecar is the enforcement point; if revocation is not checked there, a revoked token
      still works wherever it is presented.
    status: accepted
implementation:
  primary: pkg/revocation/checker.go
  test_code: pkg/revocation/checker_test.go
  test_code_status: manual
see_also:
  - smith-gray/capability-token
  - smith-gray/audit-logging
  - smith-gray/hardware-root
